Abstract: This paper proposes <code>&#91;&#91;always_tail&#93;&#93;</code> for checked tail transfers that end the caller's activation, with diagnosed rejection when unsupported. Protobuf's C++ parser and three compiler families establish use and implementation experience. A test across separate translation units on Apple Clang 21, arm64 macOS, shows ordinary dispatch exhausting the stack without optimization while <code>clang::musttail</code> and a trampoline succeed. No speed advantage is claimed. This supports developing a common contract, while a portable minimum domain, mandatory attribute semantics, and WG21's implementation-limit objection remain unresolved.
